WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.220 --> 00:00:09.480
The US faces a serious shortage of
primary care doctors — the very doctors

00:00:09.480 --> 00:00:14.460
who provide critical services for poor
and elderly patients. By 2030, there will be

00:00:14.460 --> 00:00:18.200
about 50,000 fewer primary care
physicians than we will need.

00:00:18.200 --> 00:00:22.260
And those physicians increasingly practice far
from rural areas where people who lack

00:00:22.260 --> 00:00:27.780
access to care the most tend to live. But
a new paper from AEI provides a solution:

00:00:27.780 --> 00:00:31.320
Peter Buerhaus shows that nurse
practitioners can do much of what

00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:35.969
primary care doctors do, and at a
fraction of the cost. The workforce of

00:00:35.969 --> 00:00:39.989
nurse practitioners is growing, too,
especially in under-served areas.

00:00:39.989 --> 00:00:45.300
And nurse practitioners are more likely to
treat low-income patients and people with disabilities.

00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:48.680
So what's stopping
nurse practitioners from stepping up?

00:00:48.680 --> 00:00:53.340
28 states restrict their scope of practice,
out of a concern for quality of care.

00:00:53.340 --> 00:00:57.520
well, good news: Buerhaus shows
that such concerns are unfounded.

00:00:57.520 --> 00:01:02.400
States should let nurse practitioners
do their job — disadvantaged Americans

00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:06.780
need their help, and the rest of us
need a health care system that is affordable.

00:01:07.820 --> 00:01:12.200
What do you think about Peter
Buerhaus' research? Let us know in your comments.

00:01:12.200 --> 00:01:17.380
Also, let us know what other topics
you'd like our scholars to cover in 60 seconds

00:01:17.380 --> 00:01:22.520
and be sure to LIKE and
subscribe for more research and videos from AEI.

